{{define "title"}}Agent access{{end}}
{{define "main"}}
<main class="doc stack">
  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">repositories</a> / <span class="here">docs / agents</span></p>

  <div class="doc-body">
  <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <span class="label">On this page</span>
    <a href="#token">1 · Get a token</a>
    <a href="#base">2 · Addresses and authentication</a>
    <a href="#read">3 · Read endpoints</a>
    <a href="#limits">4 · Rate limits</a>
    <a href="#errors">5 · Errors</a>
    <a href="#cli">6 · The origo command</a>
  </nav>
  <div class="stack">
    <h1 class="heading-page">Agent access</h1>
    <p class="lead">Agents use git and HTTP with a token created on this site. There is no SDK.</p>

    <section id="token" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">1 · Get a token</h2>
      <p>Go to <a href="/tokens">agent tokens</a>. Choose the repository, the scope (<strong>read</strong> unless the agent must push) and the shortest lifetime that fits. The token is shown once. Copy it immediately.</p>
      <p>Set it as <code>ORIGO_TOKEN</code> in the agent's environment. Do not commit it or put it in a prompt.</p>
      <p>Creating a token requires admin access to the repository and is done in the browser. There is no API for it.</p>
      <p>Tokens cannot be revoked. The server does not store them, so there is no list and nothing to delete. A token is valid until its lifetime ends. The maximum lifetime is {{.MaxTTL}}.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="base" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">2 · Addresses and authentication</h2>
      <p>This web interface requires a browser sign-in and does not accept tokens. The git server accepts tokens.</p>
      <pre class="scroller sample"># the git server
{{.APIBase}}

# clone, with the token as the password
git clone https://x-access-token:$ORIGO_TOKEN@{{.CloneHost}}/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;name&gt;.git

# read
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $ORIGO_TOKEN" \
  {{.RepoRead}}</pre>
      <p>API paths start with <code>/v1/repos/</code> followed by the repository <strong>identifier</strong>. The API does not accept owner and name; git clone and push do. The identifier appears in this site's URLs and does not change when a repository is renamed.</p>
      <p>Send the token as <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>, or as the password in HTTP basic auth with any username. Git uses the second form.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="read" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">3 · Read endpoints</h2>
      <p>Responses are JSON, with three exceptions: a comparison returns a unified diff, a file returns its raw content, and an archive returns a gzipped tar.</p>
      <div class="scroller">
        <table class="stackable">
          <caption>Read endpoints</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Resource</th>
              <th scope="col">Path, after <code>/v1/repos/&lt;id&gt;</code></th>
              <th scope="col">Response</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">repository</th><td data-label="Path" class="path">(none)</td><td data-label="Response">owner, name, default branch, head, size, last push time</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">branches and tags</th><td data-label="Path" class="path">/refs?prefix=refs/heads/</td><td data-label="Response">name, object id and tag target; at most 10,000</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">commit log</th><td data-label="Path" class="path">/commits?ref=&amp;path=&amp;limit=&amp;cursor=</td><td data-label="Response">newest first, 50 per page, up to 200; use the cursor for the next page</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">one commit</th><td data-label="Path" class="path">/commits/&lt;sha&gt;</td><td data-label="Response">message, parents, trailers, file and line counts</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">comparison</th><td data-label="Path" class="path">/compare/&lt;base&gt;...&lt;head&gt;?path=</td><td data-label="Response">unified diff, truncated at 1 MiB on a file boundary</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">directory</th><td data-label="Path" class="path">/tree/&lt;sha&gt;?path=&amp;cursor=</td><td data-label="Response">one level, 5,000 entries per page</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">file</th><td data-label="Path" class="path">/blob/&lt;sha&gt;</td><td data-label="Response">raw bytes; files over 50 MiB require a Range header</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">archive</th><td data-label="Path" class="path">/archive/&lt;sha&gt;.tar.gz</td><td data-label="Response">the tree at that commit</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Files and directories are addressed by <strong>object id</strong>. Resolve a path through the directory listing first. Every response names the resolved commit in the <code>Origo-Commit</code> header. A truncated response sets <code>Origo-Truncated</code>. The line limit that collapses a large file in this web interface does not apply to the API.</p>
      <p>Each endpoint covers one repository. There is no repository listing, file search or blame.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="limits" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">4 · Rate limits</h2>
      <p>Requests per minute are limited per user or agent, per server. The default is 600. An installation can set a different limit for one caller. A token shares the limit of the user who created it.</p>
      <p>Every response carries the current limit:</p>
      <pre class="scroller sample">RateLimit-Limit: 600</pre>
      <p>Over the limit, the server returns <code>429</code> with <code>Retry-After</code> in seconds. Wait that long before retrying. No additional backoff is needed.</p>
      <p>The server also returns <code>429</code> when its git worker pool is full, after waiting a few seconds for a slot. Retry those the same way.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="errors" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">5 · Errors</h2>
      <p>All errors use the same JSON shape. Branch on <code>code</code>, which is stable. <code>message</code> is human-readable. <code>details</code> depends on the code.</p>
      <pre class="scroller sample">{
  "error": {
    "code": "forbidden",
    "message": "You do not have permission to do this.",
    "details": { "reason": "token scope does not allow this action" }
  }
}</pre>
      <div class="scroller">
        <table class="stackable">
          <caption>Error codes</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Status</th>
              <th scope="col">code</th>
              <th scope="col">Retry</th>
              <th scope="col">Action</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">400</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">invalid_request</td><td data-label="Retry">no</td><td data-label="Action">Fix the request. Do not repeat it as is.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">401</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">unauthenticated</td><td data-label="Retry">no</td><td data-label="Action">Token missing, malformed or expired. Stop and ask a user for a new token.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">403</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">forbidden</td><td data-label="Retry">no</td><td data-label="Action">Stop and report <code>details.reason</code>. Covers insufficient scope and the wrong repository.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">404</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">repo_not_found</td><td data-label="Retry">no</td><td data-label="Action">The repository does not exist or is not visible to this token.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">404</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">ref_not_found</td><td data-label="Retry">no</td><td data-label="Action">Unknown reference or object id.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">409</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">non_fast_forward</td><td data-label="Retry">once</td><td data-label="Action">Fetch, rebase and push once more. Then stop.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">413</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">over_quota</td><td data-label="Retry">no</td><td data-label="Action">The repository is at its storage limit. Stop and report.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">413</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">blob_too_large</td><td data-label="Retry">no</td><td data-label="Action">Request the file in ranges of at most 50 MiB.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">429</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">rate_limited</td><td data-label="Retry">yes</td><td data-label="Action">Wait <code>Retry-After</code> seconds, then retry.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">503</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">storage_unavailable</td><td data-label="Retry">yes</td><td data-label="Action">Retry after <code>Retry-After</code>, or in a few minutes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">503</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">authorizer_unavailable</td><td data-label="Retry">yes</td><td data-label="Action">Retry in a few minutes.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">504</th><td data-label="code" class="refname">operation_timeout</td><td data-label="Retry">no</td><td data-label="Action">Timed out. Nothing changed. Request less.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Only errors with <code>Retry-After</code> are worth retrying. Retrying an expired token wastes rate limit budget.</p>
      <p>Every response carries the <code>Origo-Contract</code> header. Its value is the API contract version and changes when this page changes.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="cli" class="stack">
      <h2 class="heading-section">6 · The origo command</h2>
      <p>There is no MCP server. Origo ships a command instead, <code>origo</code>, which reads and changes one repository over the endpoints above without cloning it: list a tree, read a file, read history, compare two commits, and commit, revert, merge or cherry-pick. It prints lines, so a shell filters an answer, and every read takes <code>--json</code>.</p>
      <p>It runs where the agent runs and changes nothing on the server. It is in every Origo release archive beside the server binary, and needs four variables and no configuration file.</p>
      <pre class="scroller sample">export ORIGO_URL={{.APIBase}}
export ORIGO_TOKEN=...                         # section 1
export ORIGO_REPO=&lt;identifier&gt;                 # or &lt;owner&gt;/&lt;name&gt;
export ORIGO_AUTHOR="Name &lt;you@example.com&gt;"   # writes only

origo ls -r -n 0 | grep -i handler
origo cat internal/api/read.go
origo log -n 20 --json</pre>
      <p>The skill at <code>skills/origo/SKILL.md</code> in the Origo repository teaches an agent to use the command correctly the first time: how to hold a token bound to one repository, what each command answers without a flag, and how to read a truncated answer. Copy it wherever the agent host keeps skills.</p>
      <p>The command does not create tokens and does no administration. Create the token here and hand it over.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

  </div>
</main>
{{end}}
